.TH SAPStartSrv_upgrade_from_Filesystem 7 "24 Sep 2026" "" "SAPStartSrv"
.\"
.SH NAME
.\"
SAPStartSrv_upgrade_from_Filesystem \- upgrade from Filesystem to SAPStartSrv
.PP
.\"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\"
\fB*\fP What is the upgrade about?
.PP
It is about upgrading an HA cluster for SAP central service instances from the
Filesystem resource based setup to the simple-mount setup.
.PP
Instead several filesystems per SAP system plus additional filesystems per SAP
instance, the simple-mount setup only needs a minimum.
And these filesystems do not need to be controlled and switched-over by the Linux
cluster. All filesystems are statically mounted at all nodes at system boot,
backed by central NFS shares.
.PP
The cluster controls the start and stop of the SAP start framework of each SAP
instance and controls and monitors the SAP instances. See manual page
ocf_suse_SAPStartSrv(7) for details.
.PP
The upgrade procedure does not necessarily need downtime for the SAP central
service instances, if planned and excuted carefully. Nevertheless, it should be
done under friendly conditions.
.PP
\fB*\fP What will be changed?
.PP
This will be changed:
.br
a. Two systemd services sapping and sappong will be enabled.
.br
b. The SAP instance working directories for enqueue service and enqueue replicator
(aka ASCS and ERS) will be added to fstab on both cluster nodes, and mounted
statically. 
.br
c. The Filesystem resources for ASCS and ERS will be removed from CIB.
.br 
d. Instead, SAPStartSrv resources for ASCS and ERS will be added to the CIB.
.PP
The resulting setup will have two statically mounted directories, each backed by
one NFS share - one per SAP instance (f.e. ASCS00, ERS10). In addition it will
have the SYS directory on local disk. This is slightly different from the original
simple-mount layout, where all three directories are provided as part of one single
SAP system directory (/usr/sap/$SID/), backed by one common NFS share.
.PP
\fB*\fP How does the upgrade procedure look like at a glance?
.PP
TODO
.PP
1. Checking for clean state and collecting information.
.br
2. Setting cluster in maintenance and saving old CIB.
.br
3. Updating resource agents and enabling systemd services.
.br
4. Adding SAPStartSrv resources to CIB.
.br
5. Adapting fstab and mounting SAP instance directories.
.br
6. Removing Filesystem resources from CIB.
.br
7. Getting cluster out of maintenance and saving new CIB.
.br
8. Testing the ERS instance.
.PP
\fB*\fP What needs to be prepared upfront?
.PP
First, make yourself familiar with concepts, components and configuration of the
simple-mount HA scenario for SAP central services. Refresh your knowledge of the
Filesystem resource based HA scenario.
.PP
Next, the following information needs to be collected and documented before upgrading
a cluster:
.br
1. Names of cluster nodes
.br
2. Names of SAP instance directories
.br
3. Names of NFS server and shares for the SAP instance directories, mount options
.br
4. Names of SAP instance profiles
.br
5. Names of Filesystem resources and resource groups
.br
6. Status of packages and update repository
.br
7. Deviation of configuration from setup guide, if existing
.br
8. Matching with REQUIREMENTS section below 
.PP
Finally, prepare config templates with correct values for the given cluster.
Ideally also all needed commands are prepared in detail.
.\" TODO SAPStartSrv-upgrade-from-Filesystem-demo.8 ?
.PP
